<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shih-Ying Yeh<sup id="id1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2,3,†</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="id1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Yueh-Feng Ku<sup id="id1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="id1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ko-Wei Huang<sup id="id1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="id1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Buu-Khang Tu<sup id="id1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>National Tsing Hua University  <sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>Comfy Org Research  <sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3</span></sup>Kohaku-Lab
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Corresponding author: <span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kohaku@kblueleaf.net</span>  <math id="m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>  Code: <a href="https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/KohakuRAG" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/KohakuRAG</a>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems that answer questions from document collections face compounding difficulties when high-precision citations are required: flat chunking strategies sacrifice document structure, single-query formulations miss relevant passages through vocabulary mismatch, and single-pass inference produces stochastic answers that vary in both content and citation selection. We present KohakuRAG, a hierarchical RAG framework that preserves document structure through a four-level tree representation (document <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> section <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> paragraph <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> sentence) with bottom-up embedding aggregation, improves retrieval coverage through an LLM-powered query planner with cross-query reranking, and stabilizes answers through ensemble inference with abstention-aware voting. We evaluate on the WattBot 2025 Challenge, a benchmark requiring systems to answer technical questions from 32 documents with <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.1% numeric tolerance and exact source attribution. KohakuRAG achieves first place on both public and private leaderboards (final score 0.861), as the only team to maintain the top position across both evaluation partitions. Ablation studies reveal that prompt ordering (+80% relative), retry mechanisms (+69%), and ensemble voting with blank filtering (+1.2pp) each contribute substantially, while hierarchical dense retrieval alone matches hybrid sparse-dense approaches (BM25 adds only +3.1pp). We release KohakuRAG as open-source software at <a href="https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/KohakuRAG" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/KohakuRAG</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities across natural language processing tasks (<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>), yet suffer from hallucination, outdated knowledge, and inability to access domain-specific information absent from training data (<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) addresses these limitations by grounding LLM outputs in external knowledge sources (<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/overview.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/284;" width="648" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of KohakuRAG. <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left (Hierarchical Indexing)</span>: Documents are parsed into tree structures with sections, paragraphs (Para), and sentences (S). Sentence embeddings are computed and aggregated bottom-up to parent levels, then stored in a Vector DB. <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Center (Multi-Query Retrieval)</span>: Given a question, the Query Planner (LLM) generates multiple related queries, each retrieving Top-K results that are merged via Cross-Query Reranking. <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right (Ensemble Inference)</span>: Context and question are sent to the LLM for <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent runs; blank responses are filtered (X), and majority voting produces the final answer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The WattBot 2025 Challenge (<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) presents a compelling testbed for RAG systems: given 32 reference documents (<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>500K tokens) on AI energy consumption, systems must answer approximately 300 technical questions with <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.1% numeric tolerance, cite exact source documents, and explicitly abstain when evidence is insufficient. This combination of scale, precision, and citation requirements exposes fundamental limitations of standard RAG approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Standard RAG systems (<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) employ flat chunking that partitions documents into fixed-length segments, destroying structural boundaries and complicating precise citation tracking (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Retrieval is typically performed with a single query formulation, which misses relevant passages when user questions use different vocabulary than source documents, e.g., querying “PUE” when documents discuss “power usage effectiveness” (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>). Finally, a single LLM inference produces stochastic answers that vary across runs in both content and citation selection, with models often abstaining unnecessarily when evidence is present but difficult to locate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We present KohakuRAG, a hierarchical RAG framework that addresses each of these challenges through a matched mechanism (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical document indexing.</span> To preserve document structure, we represent documents as four-level trees (document <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> section <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> paragraph <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> sentence) and compute embeddings bottom-up via length-weighted aggregation. This provides natural citation boundaries at each granularity level while capturing compositional semantics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-query retrieval with cross-query reranking.</span> To bridge the vocabulary gap between questions and source documents, an LLM-powered query planner expands questions into multiple retrieval queries covering alternative phrasings and terminology. Cross-query reranking then leverages consensus signals, where nodes retrieved by more queries rank higher, to surface the most relevant passages.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ensemble inference with abstention-aware voting.</span> To mitigate answer instability, we aggregate <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent inference runs via majority voting. By filtering out blank responses before aggregation, we prevent conservative runs from dominating when evidence exists but is hard to locate.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our ablation studies quantify the impact of each design choice: prompt reordering (placing context before the question) yields +80% relative improvement; the retry mechanism provides +69% at low retrieval depth; and ensemble voting with blank filtering adds +1.2pp at <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=9. Hierarchical dense retrieval alone achieves competitive performance, with BM25 augmentation adding only +3.1pp, suggesting that keyword matching provides diminishing returns when retrieval structure is sufficiently rich. KohakuRAG achieves first place on both the public and private leaderboards of the WattBot 2025 Challenge with a final score of 0.861. We were the only team to maintain the top position across both evaluation partitions, demonstrating that our ensemble-based approach generalizes reliably to unseen data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A hierarchical document indexing scheme that preserves structural relationships through four-level tree representations with bottom-up embedding aggregation, enabling precise citation tracking.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An LLM-powered query planner with cross-query reranking that improves retrieval coverage by expanding questions into multiple formulations and leveraging consensus signals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An ensemble inference mechanism with abstention-aware voting that aggregates multiple runs while filtering unnecessary abstentions, the dominant error mode (26.8% of failures).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive experiments showing that hierarchical dense retrieval alone achieves competitive performance on citation-heavy tasks, and that prompt ordering and retry mechanisms contribute more than hybrid retrieval strategies.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides background on text retrieval and RAG systems. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Related Work ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> discusses related work. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Methodology ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> details our hierarchical indexing approach, retrieval strategies, and answering pipeline. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> describes our experimental setup, and Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Results ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents results and analysis. Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Conclusion ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> concludes with limitations and future directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We review the key research areas underlying our approach and formalize the WattBot 2025 Challenge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) based on the transformer architecture (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) have progressed from BERT (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and GPT-3 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) to frontier models including GPT-5 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), Gemini 3 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), Claude Opus 4.5 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), and Grok 4.1 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Open-weight alternatives have also reached frontier quality: Mistral 3 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) with 675B total parameters and 41B active, Gemma 3 (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), and GPT-oss-120B (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Despite strong reasoning capabilities, LLMs suffer from hallucination (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), outdated knowledge, and inability to access domain-specific information, motivating retrieval-augmented approaches. Vision-language models (VLMs) extend LLMs with visual understanding: Qwen3-VL (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) achieves state-of-the-art on MMMU and video understanding with native 256K-token multimodal context, while earlier work including LLaVA (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and Qwen-VL (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) established key architectural patterns. Recent surveys (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) document rapid MLLM progress enabling document understanding where figures and tables contain critical information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Text Embeddings and Retrieval</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dense retrieval represents queries and documents as vectors in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, computing relevance as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(q,d)=E(q)^{\top}E(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(q,d)=E(q)^{\top}E(d)</annotation></semantics></math> for encoder <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. DPR (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) demonstrated that contrastive learning outperforms BM25 (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), with improvements from Contriever (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and ANCE (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Modern embeddings achieve state-of-the-art on MTEB (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and its multilingual expansion MMTEB (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Notable models include Sentence-BERT (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), GTE (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), E5 (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), BGE (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), and the Jina series: Jina v3 (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) introduces task-specific LoRA adapters, while Jina v4 (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) unifies text and image embeddings through a 3.8B parameter multimodal architecture with Matryoshka representations (<span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) supporting dimensions from 128 to 2048.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dense Vector Search.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficient retrieval over large embedding collections requires approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) algorithms. HNSW (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) constructs hierarchical navigable small world graphs enabling logarithmic search complexity with high recall. The Faiss library (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) provides optimized implementations of multiple indexing strategies including inverted file indices (IVF), product quantization (PQ), and HNSW, supporting billion-scale search on both CPU and GPU. These algorithms trade exact retrieval for dramatic speedups, typically achieving <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>95% recall while reducing latency by orders of magnitude compared to brute-force search.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sparse Vector Search.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sparse methods represent documents as high-dimensional vectors with most entries zero, enabling efficient inverted index lookup. BM25 (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) remains a strong baseline, computing term-frequency statistics without training. Learned sparse methods like SPLADE (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) use neural networks to learn term weights and expand documents with semantically related terms, combining the efficiency of sparse representations with learned relevance signals. Hybrid approaches that combine dense and sparse retrieval consistently outperform either method alone (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), as lexical and semantic signals capture complementary relevance aspects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two-stage pipelines use fast first-stage retrieval followed by expensive reranking. Cross-encoders (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) jointly encode query-document pairs with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math> cost; BGE-M3 (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) provides multi-granularity reranking. Late interaction models like ColBERT (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) compute token-level MaxSim: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}(q,d)=\sum_{i}\max_{j}\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}(q,d)=\sum_{i}\max_{j}\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, balancing efficiency and accuracy. ColPali (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) extends this to multimodal document retrieval over images without OCR.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Retrieval-Augmented Generation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RAG (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) conditions generation on retrieved documents: given corpus <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and query <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, retrieve <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{q}=\text{top-}k(\{d:\text{sim}(q,d)\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{q}=\text{top-}k(\{d:\text{sim}(q,d)\})</annotation></semantics></math>, then generate <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a|q,\mathcal{D}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a|q,\mathcal{D}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Comprehensive surveys (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?????</span>) categorize systems as Naïve, Advanced (with query rewriting and reranking), Modular RAG, or Agentic RAG with autonomous retrieval decisions. Query expansion addresses vocabulary mismatch: Query2Doc (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) generates pseudo-documents, while other approaches use rewriting (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) or direct expansion (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Document segmentation impacts quality; fixed chunking loses context, while late chunking (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) encodes documents before segmentation. Our approach preserves natural structure as a tree <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with bottom-up embedding aggregation. For multimodal documents, M3DocRAG (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) shows combined text-visual retrieval improves performance. Self-consistency (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and ensemble methods (<span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) improve robustness via majority voting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Problem Formulation: The WattBot 2025 Challenge</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Document-grounded question answering spans several benchmarks with varying characteristics. Natural Questions (<span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) pairs real user queries with Wikipedia articles, requiring systems to extract short answers from long documents. TriviaQA (<span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) provides trivia questions with multiple evidence documents, testing multi-hop reasoning. PDFTriage (<span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) focuses on structured document QA with complex layouts. These benchmarks primarily evaluate answer extraction; citation accuracy and abstention behavior receive less attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The WattBot 2025 Challenge (<span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) extends document-grounded QA to AI energy estimation, requiring systems to answer technical questions about power consumption, water usage, carbon footprint, and performance characteristics. We formalize the task as follows.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task Definition.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a document corpus <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{d_{1},\ldots,d_{32}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>32</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{d_{1},\ldots,d_{32}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a question <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the system must produce a tuple <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,R,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,R,b)</annotation></semantics></math> where:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}\cup\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}\cup\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the answer, either numeric or categorical from vocabulary <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subseteq\{1,\ldots,32\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subseteq\{1,\ldots,32\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of cited document identifiers</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates abstention when evidence is insufficient</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The corpus comprises 32 reference documents (research papers, technical reports, presentation slides) totaling approximately 500K tokens, exceeding typical LLM context limits and necessitating retrieval-based approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Protocol.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The challenge imposes stringent requirements across three dimensions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Answer Accuracy</span> (75% weight): Numeric answers must fall within <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative tolerance:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{ValueScore}(a,a^{*})=\mathbf{1}\left[\left|\frac{a-a^{*}}{a^{*}}\right|\leq 0.001\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>ValueScore</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ValueScore}(a,a^{*})=\mathbf{1}\left[\left|\frac{a-a^{*}}{a^{*}}\right|\leq 0.001\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Categorical answers require exact string match after normalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Citation Precision</span> (15% weight): Measured via Jaccard similarity between predicted and ground-truth reference sets:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RefScore}(R,R^{*})=\frac{|R\cap R^{*}|}{|R\cup R^{*}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>RefScore</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RefScore}(R,R^{*})=\frac{|R\cap R^{*}|}{|R\cup R^{*}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hallucination Avoidance</span> (10% weight): Systems must explicitly abstain (<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math>) rather than hallucinate when evidence is insufficient. Incorrect answers when abstention was appropriate incur penalties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The final score combines these components:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Score}=0.75\times\text{ValueScore}+0.15\times\text{RefScore}+0.10\times\text{HallucinationScore}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Score</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>ValueScore</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>RefScore</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>HallucinationScore</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Score}=0.75\times\text{ValueScore}+0.15\times\text{RefScore}+0.10\times\text{HallucinationScore}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">requiring systems to jointly optimize retrieval coverage, answer extraction, and uncertainty quantification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss prior work on document chunking strategies, query expansion methods, and ensemble approaches for RAG systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Document Chunking and Hierarchical Indexing.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard RAG systems partition documents into fixed-length chunks for embedding and retrieval (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>). While simple to implement, fixed-length chunking destroys semantic boundaries and complicates citation tracking. Late chunking (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) addresses boundary artifacts by encoding documents before segmentation, preserving contextual information across chunk boundaries. However, late chunking still produces flat representations without explicit hierarchical structure. Our approach differs by constructing explicit tree representations that preserve document organization (sections, paragraphs, sentences) and enable precise provenance tracking through hierarchical node identifiers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Query Expansion and Multi-Query Retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single queries often lack sufficient context for optimal retrieval, motivating query expansion techniques. Query2Doc (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) generates pseudo-documents to enrich query representations. Other approaches employ query rewriting (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) or direct expansion through prompting (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). We adopt LLM-powered query planning that generates multiple semantically related queries, combined with cross-query reranking that leverages consensus signals, where nodes retrieved by multiple queries rank higher, providing implicit voting across different query formulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-Reflection and Corrective RAG.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work explores mechanisms for RAG systems to assess and improve their own outputs. Self-RAG (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) trains models to generate special reflection tokens that indicate when to retrieve, whether retrieved passages are relevant, and whether generated content is supported by evidence. CRAG (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) introduces a corrective retrieval mechanism that evaluates retrieval quality and triggers web search when initial retrieval is insufficient. Our retry mechanism shares similar motivation: when the model indicates insufficient evidence (abstention), we expand context and retry. However, our approach operates at inference time without requiring specialized training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ensemble Methods for RAG.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-consistency (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) improves reasoning by sampling multiple outputs and selecting the most common answer through majority voting. Recent surveys on LLM ensembles (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) document various aggregation strategies for combining predictions across models or runs. Our ensemble mechanism extends these ideas to RAG with reference-aware voting modes that jointly consider answer values and citation sets, with explicit handling of abstention cases where some runs indicate insufficient evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal Document Understanding.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Technical documents frequently contain critical information in figures and tables. M3DocRAG (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) demonstrates that combining text and visual retrieval improves performance on multi-page document understanding. ColPali (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) enables document retrieval directly from page images using late interaction over vision embeddings. Our framework supports both caption-based retrieval (where vision models generate textual descriptions during indexing) and direct image embedding through Jina v4 (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), enabling flexible multimodal retrieval strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present KohakuRAG, a hierarchical RAG framework designed for document-grounded question answering with precise citation requirements. Our approach addresses three key challenges: (1) preserving document structure during indexing, (2) improving retrieval coverage through multi-query planning, and (3) achieving robust answers via ensemble inference. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the complete pipeline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_top ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:234.9pt;">
<div id="alg1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:225.5pt;">
<p id="alg1.1.1" class="ltx_p">The KohakuRAG pipeline operates in three phases. First, given question <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, an LLM-powered query planner generates <math id="S4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> semantically related queries <math id="S4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that cover different phrasings and aspects of the information need. Each query retrieves top-<math id="S4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nodes via dense similarity search, and results are aggregated through cross-query reranking that prioritizes nodes retrieved by multiple queries.</p>
<p id="alg1.1.2" class="ltx_p">Second, retrieved nodes are expanded with hierarchical context, including parent paragraphs and sibling sentences, to provide the LLM with sufficient surrounding information.</p>
<p id="alg1.1.3" class="ltx_p">Third, <math id="S4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent inference runs generate answer candidates <math id="S4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates abstention. If <math id="S4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(j)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(j)}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and retries remain, we increase <math id="S4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and re-retrieve. Finally, ensemble voting with blank filtering produces the final answer.</p>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> KohakuRAG Pipeline</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, index <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{queries}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>queries</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{queries}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\text{runs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mtext>runs</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\text{runs}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,R,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,R,b)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">KohakuRAG</span>(<math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q,\mathcal{I},k,n,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q,\mathcal{I},k,n,m</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}\leftarrow\textsc{PlanQueries}(q,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">PlanQueries</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}\leftarrow\textsc{PlanQueries}(q,n)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\mathcal{R}\cup\textsc{Retrieve}(\mathcal{I},q_{i},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Retrieve</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\mathcal{R}\cup\textsc{Retrieve}(\mathcal{I},q_{i},k)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\textsc{Rerank}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rerank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\leftarrow\textsc{Rerank}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\leftarrow\textsc{ExpandContext}(\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">ExpandContext</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\leftarrow\textsc{ExpandContext}(\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})\leftarrow\textsc{Generate}(\mathcal{C},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Generate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})\leftarrow\textsc{Generate}(\mathcal{C},q)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(j)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(j)}=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and</span> retries left <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
            
            
               <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leftarrow k+\Delta k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leftarrow k+\Delta k</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="alg1.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">goto</span> line 4

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}\cup\{(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}\cup\{(a^{(j)},R^{(j)},b^{(j)})\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{EnsembleVote}(\mathcal{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">EnsembleVote</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{EnsembleVote}(\mathcal{A})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Hierarchical Document Indexing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard RAG systems employ flat chunking strategies that partition documents into fixed-length segments. While simple, this approach destroys document structure and complicates citation tracking. We instead preserve the natural hierarchy of technical documents through tree-structured indexing.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Document Parsing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a document <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a tree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{d}=(V_{d},E_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d}=(V_{d},E_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> with four levels of granularity. The root node represents the entire document. Section nodes correspond to logical divisions (chapters, numbered sections). Paragraph nodes capture coherent text blocks within sections. Sentence nodes form the leaves, containing individual sentences extracted via rule-based segmentation. Each node <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> stores its text content <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, a unique identifier following the pattern <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">doc:sec<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>:p<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>:s<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and metadata including source document ID and position indices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For documents containing figures and tables, we treat each visual element as a special paragraph node with <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attachment_type=image</span>. We employ a Vision Language Model (VLM) from the Qwen-VL family (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) to generate captions, which are stored as the node’s text content. This enables text-based retrieval of visual information while preserving the hierarchical structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bottom-Up Embedding Propagation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute embeddings in a bottom-up manner to capture compositional semantics. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the children of node <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. For leaf nodes (sentences), we directly compute embeddings using a text encoder <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{v}=E(t_{v}),\quad\text{if }\mathcal{C}(v)=\emptyset" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{v}=E(t_{v}),\quad\text{if }\mathcal{C}(v)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For internal nodes, the embedding is computed as a weighted average of child embeddings:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{v}=\frac{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}(v)}w_{c}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{c}}{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}(v)}w_{c}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{v}=\frac{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}(v)}w_{c}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{c}}{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}(v)}w_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{c}=|t_{c}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{c}=|t_{c}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the token count of child <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. This length-weighted averaging ensures that longer, more informative children contribute proportionally more to the parent embedding. The resulting embeddings at paragraph and section levels capture the aggregate semantics of their constituents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Embedding Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We support two embedding backends. Jina Embeddings v3 (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) provides 768-dimensional text embeddings with 8K context length and task-specific LoRA adapters, trained with multi-stage contrastive learning similar to GTE (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), E5 (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), and BGE (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). These models achieve state-of-the-art results on the MTEB benchmark (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). Jina Embeddings v4 (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) extends this with multimodal capability and Matryoshka representations (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) (128–2048 dimensions), enabling direct image embedding for cross-modal retrieval.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vector Storage.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Embeddings are stored using sqlite-vec, a lightweight SQLite extension for vector similarity search. Our middleware layer, KohakuVault, provides a unified interface for key-value metadata storage and vector indexing within a single database file. This design enables efficient deployment without external database dependencies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Multi-Query Retrieval</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">User questions often lack sufficient context for optimal retrieval. A query like “What is the PUE of Google’s data centers?” may miss relevant passages discussing “power usage effectiveness” or “energy efficiency metrics.” We address this through LLM-powered query planning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Query Planner.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given question <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, an LLM generates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> semantically related queries <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that cover different phrasings and aspects of the information need. The planner is prompted to produce queries that:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rephrase the original question using alternative terminology</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expand abbreviations and technical terms</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decompose compound questions into sub-queries</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Add contextual keywords likely to appear in relevant passages</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dense Retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each planned query <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we embed it and retrieve the top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> matching nodes via cosine similarity search:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}=\text{top-}k\left(\{v\in V:\cos(\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}},\mathbf{e}_{v})\},k\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}=\text{top-}k\left(\{v\in V:\cos(\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}},\mathbf{e}_{v})\},k\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Retrieval is restricted to sentence and paragraph nodes, as these contain the most specific information. Section and document nodes, while indexed, are too coarse for precise matching.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-Query Reranking.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Results from multiple queries are aggregated and reranked. While neural rerankers such as cross-encoders (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) and late interaction models (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>) offer strong performance, we employ a lightweight score-based reranking to minimize latency. For each unique node <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i}\mathcal{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i}\mathcal{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frequency</span>: <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{v}=|\{i:v\in\mathcal{R}_{i}\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{v}=|\{i:v\in\mathcal{R}_{i}\}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of queries that retrieved <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total score</span>: <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{v}=\sum_{i:v\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\text{sim}(q_{i},v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{v}=\sum_{i:v\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\text{sim}(q_{i},v)</annotation></semantics></math>, the cumulative similarity</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We support three reranking strategies:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Frequency</span>: Sort by <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{v},s_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{v},s_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> descending, so nodes retrieved by more queries rank higher</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Score</span>: Sort by <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> descending, so nodes with highest cumulative similarity rank higher</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Combined</span>: Sort by <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot\hat{f}_{v}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\hat{s}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot\hat{f}_{v}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\hat{s}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\cdot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>⋅</mo><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\cdot}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes min-max normalization</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After reranking, results are optionally truncated to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math> nodes to control context length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical Context Expansion.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieved nodes are expanded with their hierarchical context. For each matched node <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we include its parent node (providing broader context) and sibling sentences (providing local context). This expansion leverages the tree structure: a matched sentence automatically brings its containing paragraph, enabling the LLM to understand the surrounding discussion without redundant retrieval. Since multi-query retrieval often returns the same node from different queries, we deduplicate by node ID after reranking, removing cross-query duplicates before context expansion.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/context_expansion.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/223;" width="648" height="223" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Hierarchical context expansion. Given a query, we retrieve Top-K nodes across multiple granularity levels (sentences in cyan, paragraphs in green, sections in purple). Section-level nodes are filtered out as too coarse (marked with X). The remaining nodes are expanded by adding their parent nodes to provide broader context. For example, a matched sentence <span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sec1:p1:s1</span> brings its parent paragraph <span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sec1:p1</span>, while a matched paragraph <span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sec2:p2</span> brings its parent section <span id="S4.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sec2</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optional BM25 Augmentation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For queries requiring exact keyword matching (e.g., specific model names or numerical values), we optionally augment dense retrieval with BM25 (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) results. While learned sparse methods like SPLADE (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) offer improved performance, we use traditional BM25 for simplicity. Hybrid sparse-dense retrieval has been shown to provide complementary benefits (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>); the sparse results are appended after dense results without score fusion, providing complementary lexical matches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Answering Pipeline</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/retry_mechanism.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/155;" width="648" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Retry mechanism flow. When the LLM outputs <span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank=true</span> (insufficient evidence), the system increases top-<math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and re-retrieves context. This loop continues until a valid answer is produced or the retry limit is reached, at which point the system outputs a blank response.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt Construction.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieved snippets are formatted with explicit reference markers: <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[ref_id={doc_id}] {text}</span>. This format enables the LLM to directly cite sources by document ID. For documents with images, captions are included in a separate “Referenced media” section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We found that prompt ordering significantly affects answer quality. Placing the question and instructions <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> the context (rather than before) yields better results, consistent with the “lost in the middle” phenomenon (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). This effect arises from attention sink behavior (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), where initial tokens accumulate disproportionate attention mass due to softmax normalization, combined with recency bias from causal attention and rotary position encodings (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Structured Output.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM is instructed to output JSON with four fields:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer</span>: Natural language response</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer_value</span>: Extracted value (numeric or categorical)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ref_id</span>: List of source document IDs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">explanation</span>: Reasoning chain (for debugging)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">When evidence is insufficient, the model outputs <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank=true</span> to explicitly abstain rather than hallucinate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retry Mechanism.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the LLM indicates insufficient evidence (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Answering Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we retry with increased <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to provide more context. This iterative deepening allows the system to find relevant information that may have ranked just below the initial cutoff.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ensemble Inference</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single-inference answers exhibit variance due to LLM sampling stochasticity. Following self-consistency (<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>) and recent work on LLM ensembles (<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>), we improve robustness through ensemble inference with majority voting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Run Aggregation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each question, we perform <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent inference runs with temperature <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Each run produces an answer tuple <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{(j)},R^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{(j)},R^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Voting Strategies.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We support multiple voting modes:</p>
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Independent</span>: Vote on answer and references separately</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AnswerPriority</span>: First vote on answer, then collect references from matching runs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RefPriority</span>: First vote on references, then select answer from matching runs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Union</span>: Vote on answer, take union of references from matching runs</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I5.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Intersection</span>: Vote on answer, take intersection of references</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Blank Handling.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A critical design choice is handling abstention (<span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank</span>) answers. Setting <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ignore_blank=true</span> filters out blank answers before voting if any non-blank answer exists. This prevents conservative runs from dominating when evidence exists but is difficult to find. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Blank Handling. ‣ 4.4 Ensemble Inference ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the two cases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/ensemble_voting.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/254;" width="648" height="254" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Ensemble voting with blank handling. <span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1 (Mixed Responses)</span>: When non-blank answers exist among 5 runs {a, a, blank, b, a}, blank responses are filtered (X) before majority voting, yielding {a, a, b, a} with majority “a” (3 votes). <span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2 (All Blank)</span>: When all runs return blank, the system correctly abstains rather than hallucinating, preserving the hallucination score.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Multimodal Retrieval</h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:216.1pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F5.1" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:244.3pt;">
<p id="S4.F5.1.1" class="ltx_p">Technical documents often contain critical information in figures and tables (<span id="S4.F5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>). KohakuRAG supports two modes of multimodal retrieval:</p>
<p id="S4.F5.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Caption-Based Retrieval.</span>
Image captions generated during indexing are stored as paragraph nodes. Text queries naturally retrieve relevant images through their captions, which are included in the LLM prompt as text.</p>
<p id="S4.F5.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision-Enabled Retrieval.</span>
With Jina v4 embeddings, images can be directly embedded and retrieved, similar to ColPali (<span id="S4.F5.1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">?</span>). For vision-capable LLMs such as GPT-4V, Gemini, and Qwen2-VL (<span id="S4.F5.1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">??</span>), retrieved images are sent as base64-encoded content alongside text, enabling direct interpretation of visual information.</p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/image_processing.png" id="S4.SS5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:298/254;" width="298" height="254" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Dual-path image processing. <span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Caption-based retrieval via Qwen3-VL captioning and Jina v3/v4 text embedding. <span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Direct image embedding via Jina v4’s multimodal encoder.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct ablation studies to evaluate each component of KohakuRAG on the WattBot 2025 Challenge training set.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset and Corpus.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The WattBot 2025 corpus comprises 32 reference documents (research papers and technical slides) totaling approximately 500K tokens. The training set contains 41 questions with ground-truth answers and reference IDs, spanning AI energy characteristics including power consumption, water usage, carbon footprint, and performance metrics. After hierarchical parsing, our index contains 32 documents, 639 sections, 944 paragraphs, 24,565 sentences, and 280 images (275 with extractable content).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/umap_embedding.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/430;" width="648" height="430" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>UMAP visualization of Jina v4 embeddings for all indexed nodes, colored by source document. Points are differentiated by node type (documents, sections, sentences, paragraph averages, images, and full paragraphs). We annotate three types of regions: <span id="S5.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">green circles</span> mark document-dominant clusters where one paper dominates 50–99% of nodes with minor contributions from related documents; <span id="S5.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red circles</span> highlight cross-document semantic clusters where multiple documents mix, indicating shared topics (e.g., energy measurement methodologies); <span id="S5.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">blue circles</span> show image-text alignment regions where image embeddings (stars) cluster with semantically related sentences. This validates that our hierarchical embeddings preserve document coherence while enabling cross-document similarity matching.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the official WattBot scoring protocol: <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value Score</span> (75%) measures answer accuracy with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> tolerance for numerics; <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference Score</span> (15%) computes Jaccard similarity of reference IDs; <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination Score</span> (10%) rewards correct abstention. Final score: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75\times\text{value}+0.15\times\text{ref}+0.10\times\text{hallucination}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>value</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>ref</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>hallucination</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75\times\text{value}+0.15\times\text{ref}+0.10\times\text{hallucination}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Default Configuration.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unless otherwise noted, we use GPT-oss-120B as the generator, Jina v3 embeddings (768-dim), top-<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>=16 per query, 4 planner queries, combined reranking, and top-<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math>=32. Each configuration is evaluated over 3 runs with different random seeds. Statistical significance is assessed using Welch’s <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test (unequal variances); we report <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05 as significant and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.10 as marginally significant given the inherent stochasticity of LLM sampling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Retrieval Component Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate embedding models, query planning, and reranking strategies. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Retrieval Component Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents results across retrieval depths <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Retrieval component ablations (final score). Best per column in <span id="S5.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>, second best <span id="S5.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. Significance vs. best: <sup id="S5.T1.9" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup><math id="S5.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S5.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.10, <sup id="S5.T1.10" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup><math id="S5.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S5.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05, <sup id="S5.T1.11" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗∗</span></sup><math id="S5.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S5.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.01.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.12" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.12.1" class="ltx_tr">
