<th id="S5.T4.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">AnswerPriority</th>
<td id="S5.T4.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.892</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.62</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Independent</th>
<td id="S5.T4.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T4.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">0.889</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.75</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Intersection</th>
<td id="S5.T4.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.890</td>
<td id="S5.T4.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.62</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">RefPriority</th>
<td id="S5.T4.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.889</td>
<td id="S5.T4.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4.38</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Union</th>
<td id="S5.T4.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.884<sup id="S5.T4.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T4.3.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup></td>
<td id="S5.T4.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">4.62</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Ensemble performance exhibits logarithmic scaling with ensemble size, plateauing at <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=9–11. The <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ignore_blank</span> option consistently improves performance by 1.2 percentage points at <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=9 (0.882<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>0.894) by excluding abstaining runs from voting. <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AnswerPriority</span> voting achieves the best average rank (1.62) by ensuring reference consistency with the selected answer. Combining optimal configurations achieves <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.930</span> on the training set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Error Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyzed 2,583 predictions across all LLM sweep runs to identify failure patterns. Of all predictions, 75.2% were fully correct. Among the 24.8% errors, the three dominant categories are: <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unnecessary abstention</span> (26.8% of errors), where models output <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank</span> despite sufficient evidence; <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reference mismatch</span> (23.6%), where answers are correct but citations are wrong; and <span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">value selection errors</span> (22.2%), where models select incorrect but contextually plausible values. Rounding/calculation errors account for 13.3%, type mismatches for 12.2%, and unit conversion errors for only 1.6%, indicating that LLMs handle unit consistency well when the correct value is retrieved. Detailed categorization criteria and analysis are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Error Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate KohakuRAG on the WattBot 2025 Challenge test set, which consists of 282 questions split into public (66%) and private (34%) partitions. KohakuRAG achieves <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">first place</span> on the final leaderboard with a private score of <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.861</span>. Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6 Results ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes our submitted configurations and compares public vs. private score dynamics across top teams.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T5" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>WattBot 2025 test set results and leaderboard comparison. <span id="S6.T5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Our submitted configurations (all use Jina v4 with hierarchical indexing). <span id="S6.T5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Public vs. private score dynamics across top teams. Only KohakuRAG maintained #1 on both partitions.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
