</figure>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Classification Pipeline.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We developed an automated error classification that assigns each incorrect prediction to exactly one category based on a deterministic decision tree:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 1 – Abstention Check:</span> If ground truth is blank but prediction is not <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False positive</span>; if ground truth is not blank but prediction is blank <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False negative</span>; if both are blank <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 2 – Value Comparison:</span> For non-blank predictions, we compute percentage error <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=|v_{\text{pred}}-v_{\text{true}}|/|v_{\text{true}}|\times 100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>pred</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=|v_{\text{pred}}-v_{\text{true}}|/|v_{\text{true}}|\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math> and ratio <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=v_{\text{pred}}/v_{\text{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>pred</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=v_{\text{pred}}/v_{\text{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> for numeric values; case-insensitive comparison for categorical values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 3 – Numeric Error Classification:</span> For <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> (WattBot tolerance): (1) <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unit conversion</span> if <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is within <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of any power of 10; (2) <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rounding/calculation</span> if <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\leq 10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\leq 10\%</annotation></semantics></math>; (3) <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value selection</span> otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Step 4 – Reference and Type Errors:</span> If value is correct but references differ <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference mismatch</span>. If expected numeric but got text (or vice versa) <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type mismatch</span>. If both non-numeric text but different <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Categorical mismatch</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Error Distribution and Patterns</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this classification to all 641 errors yields the distribution shown in Figure <a href="#A3.F19" title="Figure 19 ‣ C.2 Error Distribution and Patterns ‣ Appendix C Error Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>.
The three dominant categories, unnecessary abstention (26.8%), reference mismatch (23.6%), and
value selection (22.2%), together account for 72.6% of all errors, suggesting focused intervention
points for system improvement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F19" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/error_distribution_bar.png" id="A3.F19.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:486/300;" width="486" height="300" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 19: </span>Distribution of error types across 641 errors from 63 sweep runs. The top three categories
(abstention, reference mismatch, value selection) account for nearly three-quarters of all errors.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False negative (unnecessary abstention)</span>: 172 errors (26.8%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference ID mismatch</span>: 151 errors (23.6%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value selection errors</span>: 142 errors (22.2%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rounding/calculation errors</span>: 85 errors (13.3%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Type mismatch</span>: 78 errors (12.2%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unit conversion errors</span>: 10 errors (1.6%)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Categorical mismatch</span>: 3 errors (0.5%)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patterns by Configuration.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond aggregate statistics, we examine how error rates vary across LLM models and retrieval depths.
Figure <a href="#A3.F20" title="Figure 20 ‣ Patterns by Configuration. ‣ C.2 Error Distribution and Patterns ‣ Appendix C Error Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> shows the overall error rate (computed as <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\text{final\_score}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>final_score</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\text{final\_score}</annotation></semantics></math>)
for each LLM and top-<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> combination.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F20" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/error_heatmap.png" id="A3.F20.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:421/351;" width="421" height="351" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 20: </span>Error rates by LLM model and retrieval depth. Grok-4.1-fast achieves the lowest error rates
across all configurations, while smaller models (GPT-5-nano) show consistently higher error rates.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F22" title="Figure 22 ‣ Patterns by Configuration. ‣ C.2 Error Distribution and Patterns ‣ Appendix C Error Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> breaks down errors into value errors (wrong answer) and reference errors
(wrong citation) by model. Notably, value error rates vary more dramatically across models than reference
errors, suggesting that answer extraction capability is more model-dependent than citation accuracy.
Figure <a href="#A3.F22" title="Figure 22 ‣ Patterns by Configuration. ‣ C.2 Error Distribution and Patterns ‣ Appendix C Error Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> shows how these error types vary with retrieval depth.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F22" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F22.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:225.5pt;"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/error_by_llm.png" id="A3.F22.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:311/171;" width="311" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 21: </span>Value vs. reference error rates by LLM. Value errors show larger variation across models.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F22.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:225.5pt;"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/error_by_topk.png" id="A3.F22.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:311/220;" width="311" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 22: </span>Error rates by retrieval depth. <math id="A3.F22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math> shows highest value errors; reference errors decrease with <math id="A3.F22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Several patterns emerge: (1) <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model capacity matters</span>: larger models (Grok-4.1-fast, Kimi-k2, Gemini-3-pro)
achieve 9–13% error rates compared to 21–25% for GPT-5-nano. (2) <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieval depth trade-offs</span>: the
relationship between top-<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and error rate is non-monotonic; <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=16</annotation></semantics></math> often outperform <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>.
(3) <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Error type differences</span>: value errors are more sensitive to model choice, while reference errors
are more stable across configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Concrete Examples and Analysis</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate our classification criteria, we provide representative examples from each error category:</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">False Negative (Unnecessary Abstention).</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “What is the carbon footprint (kgCO2eq) of training GPT-3?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth:</span> 552,000
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction:</span> <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank=true
<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The model abstained despite the answer appearing in the retrieved context. This often occurs when the exact phrasing differs (“carbon emissions” vs “carbon footprint”) or when the model is uncertain about unit conversions.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reference Mismatch.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “What is the PUE of Google’s data centers?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth value:</span> 1.1, <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refs:</span> [google2023]
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction value:</span> 1.1, <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refs:</span> [patterson2021]
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The answer is correct, but the model cited a survey paper that <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mentions</span> Google’s PUE rather than Google’s official report. Both documents were in the retrieved context.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Value Selection Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “What is the energy consumption (kWh) per inference for GPT-4?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth:</span> 0.0029
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction:</span> 0.0017 (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=41.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>41.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=41.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.59</annotation></semantics></math>)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The retrieved context contained energy figures for multiple model sizes. The model selected the value for a smaller variant rather than the full GPT-4 model. Since <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.59</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.59</annotation></semantics></math> is not close to any power of 10, this is classified as value selection rather than unit conversion.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rounding/Calculation Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “What percentage of total US electricity consumption is attributed to data centers?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth:</span> 2.5
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction:</span> 2.7 (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=8\%</annotation></semantics></math>)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The model retrieved the correct source but performed a slightly incorrect calculation or read from an outdated figure in the same document. Since <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\leq 10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\leq 10\%</annotation></semantics></math>, this is classified as rounding/calculation error.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unit Conversion Error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “How much water (liters) does training a large language model consume?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth:</span> 5,439,000
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction:</span> 5,439 (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.001=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.001=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The model confused “million liters” with “liters.” Since the ratio is within <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is classified as unit conversion error.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Type Mismatch.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span> “What is the typical GPU utilization rate in data centers?”
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground truth:</span> 30 (numeric percentage)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction:</span> “30-50%” (range string)
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis:</span> The ground truth expects a single numeric value, but the model output a range. This is classified as type mismatch regardless of whether the correct value falls within the range.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Discussion.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each dominant error category suggests different intervention strategies:
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unnecessary abstention</span> (26.8%) occurs when the model outputs <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">is_blank</span> despite sufficient context; the retry mechanism (Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Context Enhancement ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) directly addresses this by expanding context when the model abstains.
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference mismatch</span> (23.6%) indicates provenance tracking remains challenging; common causes include citing survey papers rather than original sources.
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value selection errors</span> (22.2%) arise when context contains multiple plausible values and the model selects the wrong one.
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rounding/calculation errors</span> (13.3%) are close misses indicating the system found correct information but made minor arithmetic mistakes.
<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unit conversion errors</span> are notably rare (1.6%), indicating LLMs handle unit consistency well.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Implications for RAG System Design</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our error analysis suggests several directions for improving RAG system reliability:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="A3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduce unnecessary abstention.</span> The retry mechanism with context expansion proves effective; further improvements could include confidence calibration or ensemble voting that ignores blank answers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improve reference tracking.</span> Reference mismatch errors suggest LLMs struggle with precise attribution. Potential solutions include explicit reference extraction as a separate step or constraining answers to specific retrieved documents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disambiguate similar values.</span> Value selection errors often occur when context contains multiple related values. Query planning with more specific sub-queries could help.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accept minor calculation errors.</span> Rounding errors represent fundamentally correct retrievals; evaluation metrics could distinguish between major and minor errors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model selection matters.</span> The 16-point gap between Grok-4.1-fast (9.2%) and GPT-5-nano (25.4%) underscores that LLM capability is a dominant factor, potentially more impactful than retrieval optimizations.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix presents a comprehensive analysis of the WattBot 2025 competition leaderboard,
examining the dynamics between public and private evaluation partitions. Understanding these
dynamics is crucial for interpreting competition results and provides insights into method
robustness, a key concern when deploying RAG systems in production environments where
evaluation data may differ from development conditions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Final Leaderboard</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F23" title="Figure 23 ‣ D.1 Final Leaderboard ‣ Appendix D WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> shows the official leaderboards of the WattBot 2025 Challenge. KohakuRAG (team “Kohaku-Lab”) achieved first place on both the public and private leaderboards, with scores of 0.902 and 0.861 respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F23" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F23.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/leaderboard/leaderboard_public.png" id="A4.F23.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/166;" width="648" height="166" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Public leaderboard</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F23.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/leaderboard/leaderboard_private.png" id="A4.F23.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:648/180;" width="648" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Private leaderboard (final)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 23: </span>WattBot 2025 Challenge leaderboards. (a) Public leaderboard visible during the competition. (b) Final private leaderboard revealed after the submission deadline. KohakuRAG maintained rank #1 across both partitions.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Analysis Methodology</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The WattBot 2025 competition employed a standard Kaggle-style evaluation protocol with two
distinct test partitions: a <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">public</span> partition (66% of test questions) whose scores were
visible during the competition, and a <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">private</span> partition (34%) revealed only after the
submission deadline. Importantly, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">both partitions are held-out test sets</span>: neither is
directly accessible for training or validation. The key difference is that teams can observe
public scores and iterate their submissions accordingly, while private scores remain completely
hidden until the competition ends.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This creates an interesting evaluation dynamic: the public leaderboard measures performance on
one test distribution that teams can <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">indirectly</span> optimize through submission feedback,
while the private leaderboard measures transfer to a different test distribution with no
optimization signal. A robust method should perform well on <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">both</span> partitions, as the
final evaluation considers aggregate performance across the entire test set.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the complete leaderboard data to understand:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How consistently do methods perform across the two test partitions?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What patterns distinguish teams with stable vs. volatile performance?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does KohakuRAG’s performance compare across both evaluation dimensions?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Score Transition Analysis</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The competition included 66 teams that submitted predictions evaluated on both
partitions. Table <a href="#A4.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ D.3 Score Transition Analysis ‣ Appendix D WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> summarizes the key statistics describing
score transitions from public to private evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 13: </span>Summary statistics for public-to-private score transitions across all participating teams.
The score delta (<math id="A4.T13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>) is computed as private score minus public score, where negative values
indicate performance degradation on the private partition.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T13.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T13.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T13.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="A4.T13.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T13.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></th></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Total teams</th>
<th id="A4.T13.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">66</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T13.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Mean <math id="A4.T13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> (private <math id="A4.T13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> public)</th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-0.0106</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Standard deviation of <math id="A4.T13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0452</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Median <math id="A4.T13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-0.0160</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Minimum <math id="A4.T13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-0.2130</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Maximum <math id="A4.T13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0980</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Teams with score decrease</th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">43 (65.2%)</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Teams with score increase</th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">22 (33.3%)</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T13.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T13.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Pearson correlation (public score, <math id="A4.T13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="A4.T13.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">-0.389</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several observations emerge from these statistics. First, the majority of teams
(65%) experienced score degradation on the private partition, with a mean delta of <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.0106" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0106</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.0106</annotation></semantics></math>.
This systematic decrease is expected: teams naturally gravitate toward configurations that
score well on the observable public partition, which may not transfer perfectly to the
private partition’s question distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we observe a moderate negative correlation (<math id="A4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=-0.389" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.389</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=-0.389</annotation></semantics></math>) between public scores and
score deltas. This indicates that teams with higher public scores tended to experience larger
drops on the private partition. However, this does <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> imply that achieving high
public scores is undesirable; it simply reflects that there is variance between the two
test distributions, and methods optimized (even indirectly) for one may not perfectly transfer
to the other. Figure <a href="#A4.F24" title="Figure 24 ‣ D.3 Score Transition Analysis ‣ Appendix D WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> visualizes this relationship.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F24" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/leaderboard_correlation.png" id="A4.F24.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:454/299;" width="454" height="299" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 24: </span>Relationship between public score and score delta. Each point represents a team;
KohakuRAG is marked with a red star. The negative trend reflects variance between the two
test distributions rather than indicating that high public scores are inherently problematic.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F25" title="Figure 25 ‣ D.3 Score Transition Analysis ‣ Appendix D WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a> shows the distribution of score deltas across all teams,
providing context for evaluating any individual team’s performance transition. The distribution
is left-skewed, with more teams experiencing score drops than gains.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F25" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07612v1/images/leaderboard_delta_hist.png" id="A4.F25.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:454/298;" width="454" height="298" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 25: </span>Distribution of score deltas (private <math id="A4.F25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> public) across all teams.
KohakuRAG’s delta is marked for reference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Rank Movement Patterns</h3>

<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Top-10 Teams.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the dynamics among competitive submissions, we examine the top 10 teams
from the public leaderboard and their corresponding private performance. Table <a href="#A4.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Top-10 Teams. ‣ D.4 Rank Movement Patterns ‣ Appendix D WattBot 2025 Leaderboard Analysis ‣ KohakuRAG: A simple RAG framework with hierarchical document indexing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>
presents these transitions in detail.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T14" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 14: </span>Public-to-private transition for the top 10 public leaderboard teams. The <math id="A4.T14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> column
shows score change, and Rank <math id="A4.T14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> shows position change (positive = improved). KohakuRAG (<math id="A4.T14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math>)
was the only team to maintain first place across both partitions.</figcaption>
