</figure>
<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Teams in the top rank bands (1–10 and 11–20) exhibit more negative mean deltas compared to
lower-ranked teams. The 21–30 and 51–100 bands show near-zero or slightly positive mean deltas.
This pattern has a natural explanation: lower-ranked teams had more “room to improve” on the
private partition (regression to the mean), while top teams were already near the performance
ceiling and thus more likely to show decreases. This does <span id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> suggest that targeting
mid-tier public rankings leads to better outcomes, since teams that jumped in rank still achieved
lower final scores than consistently top-performing teams like KohakuRAG.</p>
</div>
</section>
